Table~\ref{tab:perQuestionType} reports a per-question-type breakdown on the test split. The 2WikiMultihopQA question types have very different routing distributions. The \texttt{comparison} slice is almost always \texttt{naive\_enough}, with only $3$ of $89$ test rows requiring \emph{Mix}. The \texttt{bridge\_comparison} slice sits around one third. The \texttt{compositional} slice is the one in which the routing decision matters most, with $33$ of $64$ test rows requiring \emph{Mix}. The \texttt{inference} slice has only one test row and is omitted from the per-class metrics. On the \texttt{compositional} slice the ModernBERT router improves AUPRC over TF-IDF (mean $0.846$ vs. $0.787$). On \texttt{bridge\_comparison} the two routers behave qualitatively differently. At the validation-selected threshold the TF-IDF router never predicts \emph{Mix} on this slice (recall $0$), while ModernBERT recovers some examples (mean recall $0.154$, mean F1 $0.245$). The AUPRC, however, is slightly lower for ModernBERT, which means TF-IDF ranks the few positive bridge\_comparison rows above its threshold but never crosses the threshold itself.

